// Code-signing identity for the Everest app target only. Never for the
// dependencies — see project.yml's `settings.base` for why a real identity
// there breaks eight SPM packages at once.
//
// The default below is ad-hoc, so a clean clone builds on any Mac with no
// certificate and no Apple Developer account. It has a real cost: macOS binds
// Accessibility permission to the code signature, an ad-hoc signature is a
// content hash, and so every rebuild is a new identity — the switch in System
// Settings stays on while `AXIsProcessTrusted()` returns false.
//
// To keep the grant across rebuilds, create an untracked `Signing.local.xcconfig`
// next to this file (it is gitignored) containing one line:
//
//     CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = <your certificate's SHA-1>
//
// Find the SHA-1 with: security find-identity -v -p codesigning
//
// The SHA-1, not the certificate's name. Naming it sends Xcode into automatic
// provisioning even under Manual style, which then fails looking for a legacy
// "Mac Development" certificate that a free account cannot be issued.
//
// **For a release, that certificate must be `Developer ID Application`.**
// Notarisation accepts nothing else: an Apple Development certificate signs a
// build that runs fine here and comes back Invalid from the notary service.
// `scripts/make_dmg.sh` reads this same file to re-sign Sparkle's nested
// helpers, which the build leaves ad-hoc, and warns when the identity is not
// a Developer ID one. See docs/RELEASING.md.

CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Manual
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -

#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"
